==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pro-}}
From {{der|en|la|proboscis}}, from {{der|en|grc|προβοσκίς|t=elephant's trunk}} literally "means for taking food," from {{affix|grc|προ-|t1=before|βόσκω|t2=to nourish, feed|nocat=1}}, from the {{der|en|ine-pro|-}} root {{m|ine-pro|*gʷeh₃-}} from which also comes {{m|grc|βοτάνη||grass, fodder}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pɹə(ʊ)ˈbɒs(k)ɪs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|+|proboscides}}

# {{lb|en|anatomy}} An elongated tube from the head or connected to the mouth, of an animal.
## {{lb|en|entomology|malacology}} The tubular feeding and sucking organ of certain [[invertebrate]]s like [[insect]]s, [[worm]]s and [[mollusc]]s.
##* {{quote-book|en|title=The Evolutionary Biology of Flies|page=225|author=Brian Wiegmann|year=2012|passage=Unlike the '''proboscides''' of Lower brachyceran lineages, which are continuous with the head capsule and tend to dangle (Matsuda 1965), the '''proboscides''' of most cyclorrhaphan species are suspended by a membranous region and divided into three functional parts: the basiproboscis (rostrum), medioproboscis (haustellum), and distiproboscis (labellum), each of which is defined by internal muscles but also shares muscles with the other regions (Graham-Smith 1930; Lall and Davies 1971).|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=rElP5sNn6IYC&lpg=PA225&dq=%22cyclorrhaphan%22&pg=PA225#v=onepage&q=%22cyclorrhaphan%22&f=false}}
## The trunk of an [[elephant]].
# {{lb|en|informal|mildly|jocular}} A large or lengthy human [[nose]].

